add('same','results','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: Appendix A explicitly separates high-level decision making from low-level collision avoidance. The DUET details and five-dataset results in the supplement do not remove the perception/control assumptions or establish a robust physical controller. The source batch-axis router discrepancy documented below is separate from these paper results.')
add('metaexplore','results','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: Table 2, p. 4, separates original REVERIE labels from a corrected-label variant: unseen SPL is 34.03 for Meta-Explore and 40.27 for Meta-Explore*, which fixes typographical/synonym categories. The latter changes data processing as well as navigation. Appendix E, pp. 7–8, reports an image-goal Jackal demonstration; it is not a physical language-navigation experiment. The simulated VLN-CE comparison reports Conti-CMA SR/SPL/TL 41/35/10.90 versus Meta-Explore 49/38/14.88 (Table 6): improved success with longer travel. Image-goal Table 5 also includes worse hard-scene SPL despite a success benefit. The supplement therefore strengthens the recovery-cost and grounding-dependence interpretation, not a universal efficiency claim.')
n['metaexplore']['implementation']='The author project page and exact-title/author search located the paper and demonstration material but no official model repository as of 2026-10-22. The ten-page official supplementary PDF was obtained; methods, analyses and results on pp. 1–9 were read (the remaining page contains references). No model/loss/runtime source behavior is claimed. The source gap leaves precise implementation equivalence and reproducibility uncertain.'
n['metaexplore']['repo_status']='Official model repository not located in bounded project/title/author search; main and relevant supplementary methods reviewed.'
source('metaexplore','https://rllab-snu.github.io/projects/Meta-Explore/doc.html','Author project; paper and demonstration links checked for code')

add('landmark_rxr','method','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: pp. 1–3 specify an RCM-like recurrent baseline and chunk granularity controls. The annotation datasheet, pp. 5–8, describes 30 annotators and about 2,700 hours, with some original English RxR instruction errors corrected. Its claim contrasting FGR2R as heuristic should be read narrowly: FGR2R uses heuristic linguistic segmentation but its released chunk-to-viewpoint alignment includes human annotation. Human landmark alignment and oracle-reset evaluation remain distinct from autonomous recovery.')
add('embodiedqa','method','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: pp. 1–2 describe functional-program question generation, uniqueness constraints and entropy/count filtering; nine generator templates do not mean nine types were used in every main experiment. Page 3 pretrains visual features with semantic segmentation, depth and reconstruction, using Adam at 1e-3, batch 20 and five epochs over 100,000 images. The QA component attends to the final five frames. Page 4 contrasts human versus shortest-path views: worse answer rank on human routes is evidence of view-distribution mismatch, not worse human navigation ability.')
add('aiuta','results','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: the target image is available to the user, not the agent (p. 1). The human study covers 20 participants and 40 episodes, two per participant; failure to find a significant human-versus-simulated-user difference is not an equivalence proof (p. 5). Three component times on an RTX 4090 are reported as 11.3, 8.29 and 6.13 seconds over 20 episodes (p. 4), without an executed timing reproduction here. IDK-VQA uses 102 images and three annotators (p. 6); robustness around a tuned threshold does not establish probability calibration. The prompts request information gain, but the inspected routine does not explicitly optimize a probabilistic information-gain objective. Dialogues are capped at four rounds (p. 9).')
add('ieve','results','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: Appendix pp. 1–2 factorizes uncertainty under specified distance/match-count assumptions, without a general history-dependent guarantee. Table 2 normalizes TP+FN and TN+FP separately, while the printed algebra also sums all four as one; those conventions should not be conflated. The supplement describes a linear switching boundary from 60 matches at 2 m to 100 at 4 m, whereas the inspected active source path uses piecewise thresholds around 3 m. Page 4 collision handling marks the obstructed front cell untraversable and previously visited cells traversable. These scoped heuristics do not establish safe verification under localization errors.')
add('embodiedgap','method','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: Appendix A supplies the RDP details: LongCLIP RGB/text features plus depth and a point goal, five RGB tokens of width 512, GRU history, four previous actions and relative coordinates. Diffusion predicts eight future actions and executes four; a heuristic stops when action magnitudes are below 0.1 or progress exceeds 0.8. Training is reported as about two days on four RTX 4090s, batch eight and lr 1e-4. Fall/stuck definitions and controller choices are operational benchmark components, not abstract navigation correctness.')
add('embodiedgap','results','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: Appendix C, p. 4, reports a 14-episode Unitree Go2 CMA test improving SR from 7.14% to 28.57%, with a custom STOP probability threshold above 1e-4 because the transferred policy did not stop robustly. This small physical test is not real-world validation of RDP. Evaluation is restricted to egocentric methods, and the Jetbot camera is raised in simulation (Fig. 14). These conditions matter when interpreting embodiment generalization.')

add('explore_confident','method','FULL-VERSION APPENDIX CHECK: arXiv v2, PDF p. 15, proves that the trajectory-minimum score exceeds a calibrated threshold exactly when every step retains the true answer in its prediction set. Thus the simultaneous-coverage event carries to any stop within that fixed scenario trajectory, under the scenario exchangeability and calibration assumptions in the main paper. This is an answer-set statement, not a guarantee of collision-free control, accurate localization or finite-time singleton termination. The same appendix uses a cropped explored-view update and frontier weights exp(SV + SV_normal) with a 3 m lookahead along frontier normals. The released repository audit below still did not locate the CP calibration/intersection path.')
add('explore_confident','results','FULL-VERSION APPENDIX CHECK: pp. 16–17 find that extra exploration does not reliably help small-object state questions, and report no gain for identification questions requiring stronger semantic location inference. A view-orientation heuristic raises reported success from 58.4% to 59.3%; replacing the VLM raises it to 65.3% with LLaVA 1.6 or 73.9% with GPT-4V. These are separate component changes, not evidence that the stopping mechanism itself caused all gains.')
source('explore_confident','https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.15941v2','Complete 17-page author version; appendices A–C pp. 15–17 reviewed')

add('knowno','results','APPENDIX SCOPE: pp. 17–19 constrain hardware scenarios to avoid visually confusable detections; some challenging mobile tasks use known object locations and scripted execution policies. Figures A9–A11 retain multiple acceptable answers in some scenarios. Calibration concerns the specified scenario/label distribution and successful high-level option selection with the stated help assumptions; it does not independently certify the learned detector or low-level controller.')
add('vsi_bench','method','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: pp. 1–3 select 150 ARKitScenes, 50 ScanNet++ and 88 ScanNet videos; suppress rare/small objects and ambiguous geometry; and construct route-plan questions as filling turn directions in human-specified routes. Post-answer self-explanations are elicited after a prediction, unlike pre-answer CoT. Error attribution covers 163 selected errors with fractional assignment to overlapping categories; this is an interpretive diagnostic, not a causal intervention on internal computation. Cognitive maps use a 10 by 10 grid; all category names are supplied for map visualization, while the relative-distance intervention supplies only question-mentioned categories.')
add('vsi_bench','results','SUPPLEMENT CHECK: Table 6 reports relative-distance accuracy 36→42 for LLaVA-Video-72B and 40→32 for 7B with cognitive maps; its nearby “8%” description does not match the first row difference. Table 8 gives video-first 48.8 versus question-first 46.3, despite a reversed sentence in the prose. Tables, not the conflicting prose, determine those comparisons. Humans can replay videos without a time limit; a second 560-question human subset was selected to match model full-set performance. Open models receive 32 frames, GPT-4o 16, Gemini native video (Table 9). These protocol differences and selection procedures constrain cross-model and human comparisons. None is an executed navigation result.')

